  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 416 PM CDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nLATE AFTERNOON THROUGH TONIGHT:\n\nAs of 330 PM... Storms continued over central and southern \nMinnesota in the warm sector of a nascent low pressure system \ncentered near STC. The warm front extended eastward roughly \nalong MN/WI-70 while a cold front was farther west over \nsouthwest Minnesota. Temperatures were in the upper 50s and low \n60s over western Wisconsin into central Minnesota with dewpoints\nin the 50s. The 18Z MPX sounding revealed two elevated mixed \nlayers which were keeping the eastward extent of convection \nlimited. Hodographs over east-central Minnesota and northwest \nWisconsin were supportive of supercells capable of large hail \nand tornadoes. The main question is whether storms can become \nsurface-based. Storms are expected to lift northeastward into \nthe Brainerd Lakes and northwest Wisconsin late this afternoon \nand this evening. Areas of northwest Wisconsin and adjacent \nportions of east-central Minnesota saw partial sunshine this \nafternoon which will aid in storm development and maintenance.\n\nPrecipitation over north-central Minnesota will persist this \nevening in response to the strengthening low pressure. Look for \nthat precipitation to persist and propagate eastward over the \nArrowhead by late evening.\n\nPartial clearing is expected as the precipitation dwindles \ntonight.\n\nTHURSDAY THRU THE WEEKEND:\n\nBehind the departing cold front tonight, a cooler and drier air\nmass will sweep into our region for Thursday. High temperatures\nwill trend noticeably cooler and near to slightly above \nnormal;, generally topping out in the upper 40s to middle 50s. \nPartial clearing is expected early, particularly south of US-2. \nBy late morning, cold air advection atop modest warming should \npermit development of a broad shallow cumulus field. Gusty west \nto northwest winds will make it feel a bit brisk throughout the \nday. These winds will gradually diminish as we head through the \nafternoon and cold air advection weakens, setting the stage for \na quiet and chilly evening with lows dipping into the 20s.\n\nQuiet and pleasant weather holds across the area on Friday as \nthe surface high pressure slides to our east. Winds will be \nlighter, and temperatures will quickly rebound into the upper \n40s to upper 50s under plentiful sunshine. Highs Friday will be \n5 to 12 degrees above normal. As we head into the weekend, \nreturn flow will pull even warmer air northward, resulting in \ntemperatures climbing back to ten to twenty degrees above \nnormal. Afternoon highs will reach the mid 50s to upper 50s and \nlower 60s Saturday and Sunday, while overnight lows remain well \nabove freezing.\n\nA warm front will slowly lift northward across the Midwest into\nsouthern Minnesota and southern Wisconsin by Sunday morning. \nIsentropic ascent north of the front enhanced by a northeastward\npropagating shortwave trough will bring a chance of showers to \nareas south of US-2 in Minnesota and Wisconsin. Rain amounts \nshould be light.\n\nEARLY NEXT WEEK:\n\nBy Monday and Tuesday, the chance for widespread precipitation \nreturn as a larger and better organized storm system moves into \nthe central CONUS. Models are split on how that system will \nevolve. The latest GFS points to a cutoff low developing over \nthe central Plains while the ECMWF features a more progressive \ntrough lifting northeastward across the Upper Midwest. Cloud \ncover and periods of rain will keep conditions somewhat dreary, \nthough temperatures are expected to remain on the mild side for \nlate October. Forecast models suggest that this system could \nbring a beneficial soaking rain to much of the region before \nprecipitation gradually tapers off toward the middle of next \nweek.",
